I still like it.
At a certain point,

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:36.633
you might realize

00:24:36.633 --> 00:24:38.320
that you've been wearing the
same clothes for a long time.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:39.610
mythical.com. can help you with that.

00:24:39.610 --> 00:24:40.850
Wrap your boys.

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:42.360
Let's get back into some questions.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:43.193
Hit it.

00:24:43.193 --> 00:24:44.040
Okay.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:47.350
Now again, you haven't suffered
from this as much as I have

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:50.231
and I'm using the term suffered loosely.

00:24:50.231 --> 00:24:55.231
But because my story was very
much my intellectual journey

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:57.740
and I questioned some foundational things

00:24:57.740 --> 00:24:59.030
about the Christian faith,

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:02.509
of course, people who know
and care about these things

00:25:02.509 --> 00:25:07.509
have suggested many,
many, many, many resources

00:25:08.155 --> 00:25:09.853
for me to check out.

00:25:11.488 --> 00:25:13.840
And I wanna talk about,

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:15.290
I wanna hear a book report.

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:18.280
Listen, how do I want to get into this

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:21.070
because people have suggested websites,

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:24.640
articles, YouTube videos, books

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:29.640
and all by people who
are probably, definitely

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:32.090
many of them but probably most of them

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:33.580
are smarter than me.

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:37.303
Definitely most of them are
more well studied than me.

00:25:37.303 --> 00:25:39.487
They haven't been making YouTube videos

00:25:39.487 --> 00:25:42.570
almost exclusively for a decade.

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:46.450
They've been studying these things

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:48.750
and so the people are pointing to me,

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:51.160
pointing me to all these
different resources.

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:56.160
Now, I'm not then taking all the resources

00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:57.990
and staying up every night

00:25:57.990 --> 00:25:59.880
and just going through all the information

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:01.980
and sort of reading everything

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:03.530
that everyone is sending to me deeply.

00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:05.453
And I have a few reasons for that.

00:26:07.247 --> 00:26:11.150
The first is that, with a few exceptions,

00:26:11.150 --> 00:26:16.150
the general arguments that
people are kind of pointing me to

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:17.373
and the resources,

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:20.970
whether I've looked at those
things specifically before

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.677
or things very much like them,

00:26:22.677 --> 00:26:24.580
I kind of just want to

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:26.990
and this is a little bit
of a defense of my process

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:27.920
which I said I didn't want to do

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.609
but I just feel like I have to say it.

00:26:30.609 --> 00:26:33.566
I didn't come to any of my
conclusions lightly, right?

00:26:33.566 --> 00:26:37.030
As I said earlier, I wasn't trying

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:39.480
to get myself out of the faith.

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:40.860
Leaving the faith,

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:44.130
was the scariest possible
proposition for me.

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:44.963
Right?

00:26:44.963 --> 00:26:46.050
There was so much there for me

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:50.340
and it was a very slow
process over like 10 to 15,

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:51.860
about 15 years

00:26:53.824 --> 00:26:56.060
and it was based on looking

00:26:56.060 --> 00:26:59.260
at a lot of these types of
resources on both sides.

00:26:59.260 --> 00:27:01.410
I know I kind of listed out some resources

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.650
when I did my story and
people had a lot to say

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:06.130
about what that indicated.

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:06.963
Those weren't.

00:27:06.963 --> 00:27:07.796
That wasn't it.

00:27:07.796 --> 00:27:08.950
That wasn't the only thing I looked at.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:11.560
That was just a small
sampling of some things

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:12.470
that I found compelling

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:15.040
but I don't remember
everything that I looked at.

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:17.170
But I didn't come to any of
those conclusions lightly

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:20.287
so it isn't like, "Oh have
you been to this website

00:27:20.287 --> 00:27:21.607
"because they've got all the answers

00:27:21.607 --> 00:27:22.440
"that you're looking for?"

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:25.890
It's like, I probably
have seen those answers

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:27.270
and processed those answers

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:31.740
and in the end, after a
layman's investigation

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:33.622
over the course of 15 years,

00:27:33.622 --> 00:27:35.270
came to the conclusion

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:39.070
that I just didn't find those
things compelling, right.

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:42.100
So that I'm not super
like chomping at the bit

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:43.474
to get into 'em.

00:27:43.474 --> 00:27:46.960
But I wanna talk a little more personally

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:50.450
about why I have a kind of an issue

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:53.990
with the whole enterprise
of like apologetics

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:57.000
and these kinds of resources.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:59.630
It seems to me that one of the
things that people are doing

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:01.889
is they're saying, "Hey Rhett,

00:28:01.889 --> 00:28:04.395
"here's a really smart person

00:28:04.395 --> 00:28:07.240
"who knows a whole lot
more about this than you do

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.987
"and they've got a really
compelling book about it.

00:28:09.987 --> 00:28:11.250
"Check it out."

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:14.490
So there's a tendency, as
I talked about in my story,

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:18.140
to find a smart person, right.

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:21.230
Find a smart person who
believes what you believe

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:22.840
and use that as justification

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:25.020
that it's cool and reasonable

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:26.820
to believe what you believe.

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:28.760
And so you kind of get into this,

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:31.930
appealing to smart people game.

00:28:31.930 --> 00:28:34.410
And I don't know how to say this.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.911
I'm trying to be as charitable as possible

00:28:37.911 --> 00:28:42.911
but I don't think that this
is a really fruitful game

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:49.330
for certain kinds of Christians to play

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:51.540
because when you get,
depending on the subject,

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:52.900
once you get into the,

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:56.750
hey here's a smart person who
believes what I believe game,

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:59.321
you're gonna find pretty quickly

00:28:59.321 --> 00:29:01.360
that you're gonna get outnumbered

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:03.430
by the smart people on the
other side of that issue

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:05.600
that you're trying to get people to see.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.573
Take evolution as an example, right.

00:29:08.440 --> 00:29:11.770
The vast majority of biologists,

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:13.680
the vast majority of people

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:18.680
who have given their lives
to studying, you know biology

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:21.763
believe in common ancestry.

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:25.630
So when you find a really smart scientist

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:27.920
who doesn't believe in it and be like hey,

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:31.140
it's like well I can find
a hundred smart scientists

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:32.520
who disagree with him.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:36.640
So playing that game kind of
defeats itself after a while.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:37.940
Does that make sense?

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:38.793
Yeah, yeah.

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:42.949
But I see it done so
often and I also did it

00:29:42.949 --> 00:29:46.705
but I think that there's very
few issues that I talked about

00:29:46.705 --> 00:29:50.610
in my journey, whether you're
talking about archeology

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:53.300
or whether you're talking
about New Testament scholarship

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:54.990
where that wouldn't be the case.

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:58.354
Now interestingly, the closer
and closer you get to Jesus

00:29:58.354 --> 00:30:01.649
you know when you get into
like New Testament scholarship,

00:30:01.649 --> 00:30:03.910
a lot of people who
have devoted their lives

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:06.080
to the study of the New Testament,

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:08.400
there's a lot, you have to
be a certain kind of person

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:10.680
to get to that point in the first place

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:13.140
so the proportion of people
who disagree about that

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.650
is a little bit different
than say evolutionary biology.

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:17.500
Does that make sense?

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:18.930
Definitely.

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:21.380
But you do have plenty
of people who got into it

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:23.200
and dug into it very deeply,

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:24.970
who came out on the other side and said,

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:26.940
oh I got into this because of my love

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:29.350
for an interest in Jesus

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:31.410
and then when I started
seeing the realities,

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:34.090
I actually was very
troubled and moved away.

00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:36.580
A lot of people say go to seminary,

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:37.890
there's one of two options.

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.740
Either you're gonna
become a deeper believer,

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:42.150
you're gonna you know
your faith is gonna deepen

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:43.920
or you're gonna leave it.

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:45.340
It's not gonna stay the same.

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:50.340
But when you talk about
Christian apologetics in general,

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:51.851
something I thought about recently

00:30:51.851 --> 00:30:53.950
because I have been watching

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:58.950
a lot of these podcasts
and going, I still I love,

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:02.500
I'm kind of genuinely
interested in this stuff

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:05.090
so out of my own interest,
I still read these articles

00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:07.973
and look at these things to some degree.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:11.210
But one of the things
that has kind of struck me

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:15.750
about just the enterprise
of Christian apologetics

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:20.750
is that the whole idea is that you can use

00:31:21.714 --> 00:31:23.850
basically logic and reason

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:26.550
to make a defense of the Christian faith,

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:29.813
to make it seem reasonable and logical.

00:31:31.404 --> 00:31:36.404
But it feels like, and take
the issue of the resurrection

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.900
which is kind of the linchpin
of the whole thing right.

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:42.230
So there's plenty of people
who have written books

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.810
and I've read them some of them

00:31:44.810 --> 00:31:49.080
about why the basically
the best explanation

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:51.700
of the events around Jesus' resurrection

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:55.430
is that he actually bodily
raised from the dead

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:56.610
explains the empty tomb

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:58.280
and explains the start
of the early church,

00:31:58.280 --> 00:31:59.800
yada yada yada right.

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:00.823
heard the arguments.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:05.810
But I find it interesting
that the best defense

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:09.360
of the Resurrection which is a miraculous,

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:14.050
unbelievable, unreasonable,
illogical event

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:16.150
is to use logic and reason

00:32:16.150 --> 00:32:20.160
to try to make it seem
like it actually happened.

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:23.510
To me, the resurrection is
the ultimate test of faith.

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:26.200
It is saying I'm gonna believe something

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:27.500
that is unbelievable.

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:30.390
Isn't that what makes it a
beautiful thing to believe

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:31.930
from the standpoint of faith?

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:34.110
But if it really happened,
everything around,

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:39.110
you could have logical,
historical, philosophical,

00:32:41.013 --> 00:32:43.574
cultural convert analysis of it.

00:32:43.574 --> 00:32:47.980
Right, but I feel like all the historical,

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:50.440
logical analysis of it,

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:51.680
from the standpoint of someone

00:32:51.680 --> 00:32:53.580
who's trying to argue the resurrection,

00:32:53.580 --> 00:32:56.700
I think they're very much
overstating their case

00:32:56.700 --> 00:32:59.430
because I think that it's like again,

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:01.300
I told you I knew somebody
who wrote a term paper

00:33:01.300 --> 00:33:04.350
explaining why the resurrection happened

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:06.740
and I feel like they definitely

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:08.830
there's some logical leaps in there

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:11.363
and they're justifying
something that is okay,

00:33:11.363 --> 00:33:12.734
it could have happened.

00:33:12.734 --> 00:33:16.000
The best you can get to
is it could have happened.

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:18.120
If you believe that
resurrection is possible,

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:20.177
it is possible that Jesus resurrected.

00:33:20.177 --> 00:33:23.380
But what I'm saying is that,

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:25.300
to me it feels like you take the magic

00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:26.915
and the beauty out of something.

00:33:26.915 --> 00:33:29.390
And I'm just using the
resurrection as an example

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:33.530
but what I'm saying is
that post-enlightenment,

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:35.850
all this very super reductionist thinking

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.470
that people are applying to the faith,

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:41.612
to God in his action in the world

00:33:41.612 --> 00:33:44.854
and people's relationship with him,

00:33:44.854 --> 00:33:49.854
to me the reductionism takes
the faith and the supernatural.

00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:52.840
We're not talking about reasonable things.

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:57.180
We're talking about God,
this external personal force

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:58.970
moving into the physical world

00:33:58.970 --> 00:34:01.550
and doing things that
defy all logic and reason.

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:04.050
So when you start trying to put your logic

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:08.260
and put God into the
reasonable and logical box,

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:09.620
you're kind of taking away

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:11.777
some of his attributes in my mind,

00:34:11.777 --> 00:34:13.083
which leads me to,

00:34:14.084 --> 00:34:17.850
I personally think the most
intellectually defensible

00:34:20.075 --> 00:34:22.770
Christian position is something called

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:25.000
biblical presuppositionilism right,

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:28.210
which is basically you just
start with the presupposition

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.030
that the Bible is true

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.210
and that everything flows from that.

00:34:32.210 --> 00:34:33.350
Now, do I find that compelling?

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:34.183
No.

00:34:34.183 --> 00:34:35.016
Do I subscribe to that?

00:34:35.016 --> 00:34:35.849
No.

00:34:35.849 --> 00:34:37.750
But it I can understand it

00:34:37.750 --> 00:34:39.620
and I find that the
people who kind of start

00:34:39.620 --> 00:34:41.780
with this presuppositionalist viewpoint

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:46.740
have a lot less trouble getting
into this tit-for-tat logic

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:48.710
and reason argument back and forth

00:34:48.710 --> 00:34:49.543
because it's just like

00:34:49.543 --> 00:34:51.450
I don't have to deal with your arguments

00:34:51.450 --> 00:34:54.160
because it's I just accepted as true.

00:34:54.160 --> 00:34:57.070
I just feel like the enterprise
of Christian apologetics

00:34:57.070 --> 00:35:00.080
for a lot of people, if you
go into it with an open mind

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:02.000
and an open heart and you kind of say,

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:04.920
I'm gonna follow where that
wherever the truth leads.

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:08.610
I think one of the reasons
that so many people like me

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:10.963
when they take on that particular,

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:14.490
they go down that path
they end up where I'm at

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.010
is because exactly what I said

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:18.280
it's because you're taking and
you're applying logic to it

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:19.460
and if you're gonna do that,

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:21.770
you're going to end up in a
logical and reasonable place

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:24.503
which I don't believe is a place of faith.

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:28.190
Does that make sense?

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:29.023
Yeah.

00:35:31.526 --> 00:35:34.086
I'm not gonna add anything to it.

00:35:34.086 --> 00:35:37.350
Okay.

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:39.780
Okay, well, I didn't wanna
spend too much time talk.

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:41.930
Again, I don't I like
talking about that stuff

00:35:41.930 --> 00:35:44.340
but I don't want it to be our podcast.

00:35:44.340 --> 00:35:46.930
So let's talk about some
more personal things.

00:35:46.930 --> 00:35:49.330
Let's go back to as
personal as we possibly can.

00:35:51.154 --> 00:35:53.210
And to make this even more personal,

00:35:53.210 --> 00:35:56.130
I'm gonna say that this question came from

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:58.590
just say the name, a man named Daniel,

00:35:58.590 --> 00:35:59.500
a man named Daniel.

00:35:59.500 --> 00:36:03.920
I almost said Damniel, my
character's named Daniel.

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:05.470
He likes to have fun.

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:06.970
What are your takes on miracles

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:09.743
such as healing and unexpected blessings?

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:12.837
And another question,
I don't know if this is

00:36:12.837 --> 00:36:14.620
I think I just grabbed
this from somewhere else.

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:16.770
Have you ever had a supernatural
experience and if so,

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:19.440
how would such an experience
fit into your worldview, tell?

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:20.273
Okay.

00:36:20.273 --> 00:36:21.600
Have we ever had a--

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:22.700
Lots of people ask this question

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:23.930
Supernatural experience.
How do you,

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:27.570
because a lot of people
said God moved in my life

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.650
in an undeniable supernatural way

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:33.780
and therefore, regardless
of what you're gonna say

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:37.128
about the foundation of the faith

00:36:37.128 --> 00:36:38.890
it's so real and so personal

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:40.330
and I've seen with my own eyes

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:42.290
or I've experienced something

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:44.340
that is undeniably supernatural

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:45.550
and God's movement in my life

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:47.430
and therefore I'll never depart from him.

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:49.458
How do you relate to that?

00:36:49.458 --> 00:36:51.936
Oh you were asking me?

00:36:51.936 --> 00:36:54.586
I thought you were restating
the question in general.

00:36:55.824 --> 00:37:00.240
Well, I didn't really hear
your restating a question.

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:01.312
'cause I was thinking of if

00:37:01.312 --> 00:37:06.312
if I've ever experienced
something miraculous,

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:14.930
I don't remember ever
experiencing anything miraculous.

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:17.054
I think in our particular
church it was like,

00:37:17.054 --> 00:37:21.410
we weren't taught to expect miracles.

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:23.420
It wasn't a part of the,

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:25.440
there weren't healings

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:27.852
associated with our church services.

00:37:27.852 --> 00:37:31.720
That wasn't the denomination we came from.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:33.390
Yeah, we pursue it to something

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:36.490
you might call dispensationalism

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:40.020
which is the idea that miraculous activity

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:43.020
by the Holy Spirit coincides

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:44.270
with the dispensation right.

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:48.760
So a dispensation it
like so when obviously,

00:37:48.760 --> 00:37:50.180
there's all kinds of miracles in the Bible

00:37:50.180 --> 00:37:51.840
because A, Jesus is doing them,

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:54.090
so you've got God in the
flesh, they're doing miracles.

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:56.470
And then you've got all
these early church miracles

00:37:56.470 --> 00:37:58.620
when the Holy Spirit is
basically coming on people

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:00.550
at Pentecost and then a few years after.

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:01.419
But God doesn't do

00:38:01.419 --> 00:38:02.252
that anymore.
But God doesn't

00:38:02.252 --> 00:38:04.690
do that anymore because
he doesn't need to.

00:38:04.690 --> 00:38:06.750
That's kind of, but that doesn't mean

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:09.055
that occasionally, God's
gonna answer a prayer

00:38:09.055 --> 00:38:10.940
but it'll be one of those things it's like

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:13.010
did this person get better from cancer

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:14.280
because the church prayed

00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:16.380
or was it just, it just happened

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:18.170
or was it the medical team?

00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:20.220
But it wasn't like
somebody's gonna come in

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:22.930
and like grow hair of a different color

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:24.720
or their legs are gonna get longer,

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:27.240
they're gonna stand up from
being you know disabled

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:28.930
from for 10 years.

00:38:28.930 --> 00:38:30.690
Like that didn't happen in our church,

00:38:30.690 --> 00:38:32.410
but I do understand that it happens

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:33.580
in many people's traditions.

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:37.050
But based on that teaching,
I never really looked for it.

00:38:37.050 --> 00:38:38.300
I never expected it.

00:38:38.300 --> 00:38:40.910
I've been thinking like
a corollary question

00:38:40.910 --> 00:38:43.260
people have asked it's like

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:45.010
what about answered prayers?

00:38:45.010 --> 00:38:50.010
You know, if you wanna put
that like in a near miracle

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:52.330
or like in the miracle category,

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:55.500
I guess they're like miracle light.

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:56.386
That sounds like a beer.

00:38:56.386 --> 00:38:58.438
Well, it sounds like a spread.

00:38:58.438 --> 00:39:00.270
Miracle Whip.

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:05.270
But I when I was thinking
back, my memory's fuzzy

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:10.970
but my prayer life was not that vibrant

00:39:12.830 --> 00:39:17.320
and I don't recall ever
asking God for something

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:20.163
that I didn't think he could give me.

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:22.686
You didn't wanna be unreasonable.

00:39:22.686 --> 00:39:26.183
I didn't wanna ask for too much.

00:39:27.060 --> 00:39:29.770
That's but when you're taught how to pray,

00:39:29.770 --> 00:39:34.040
it's like you got adoration,
you got confession,

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:35.470
you got Thanksgiving

00:39:35.470 --> 00:39:38.240
and then if you haven't fallen
asleep by that point, you got

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:40.174
supplication, ACTS.
Supplication, ACTS.

00:39:40.174 --> 00:39:44.603
And I never got, I just
I had this feeling that

00:39:44.603 --> 00:39:49.020
I just never had, I didn't
wanna call God on the carpet.

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:49.853
I don't know.

00:39:49.853 --> 00:39:51.420
It's like I didn't want
to be proven wrong.

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:54.343
Maybe that was part of it
deep down subconsciously.

00:39:56.876 --> 00:39:57.709
I had a lot of again

00:39:57.709 --> 00:39:59.090
I don't want to go back to the guilt thing

00:39:59.090 --> 00:40:01.500
but like I knew that I didn't really

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:05.340
have a great prayer life
and I put that on me.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:10.120
But I don't remember
ever expecting miracles

00:40:10.120 --> 00:40:12.450
or really expecting healing you know.

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:16.180
I especially like after
college in the church

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:17.474
that I was involved in there

00:40:17.474 --> 00:40:21.580
it was a different strain of Christianity

00:40:21.580 --> 00:40:25.210
and it you know they
started, in that church

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:27.110
we believe that like the people

00:40:27.110 --> 00:40:32.110
who were gonna be saved had
been chosen ahead of time

00:40:32.290 --> 00:40:35.530
which means that others
were chosen actively

00:40:35.530 --> 00:40:37.350
for damnation ahead of time

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:41.450
and there's biblical passages
that seem to say that.

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:42.790
A lot more people believe that

00:40:42.790 --> 00:40:44.993
than you would think.

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:49.875
But it was very and you know we

00:40:49.875 --> 00:40:52.870
we joked and called
ourselves "the frozen chosen"

00:40:52.870 --> 00:40:57.123
because it wasn't a dynamic
experimental expectation.

00:40:58.880 --> 00:40:59.713
It was pretty heady.

00:40:59.713 --> 00:41:00.546
That God was...

00:41:00.546 --> 00:41:01.379
It was pretty heady.

00:41:01.379 --> 00:41:03.970
God wasn't gonna show up
and do something crazy

00:41:03.970 --> 00:41:05.310
because he didn't need to.

00:41:05.310 --> 00:41:07.410
He had already he'd been
there and done that.

00:41:07.410 --> 00:41:08.750
You either believe it or don't.

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:09.583
Right.

00:41:09.583 --> 00:41:11.350
But don't don't demand it.

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.380
Who are you to demand
that God do something?

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:16.260
So this was the environment

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:21.080
that I operated in and
I don't really recall

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:24.870
experiencing a miracle.

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:27.109
One of my mom's friends who was like,

00:41:27.109 --> 00:41:28.700
she was she was one of those

00:41:28.700 --> 00:41:30.740
what I felt like more the spiritual like

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:35.120
in touch with things that
she that are ineffable

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:36.640
and she was also a Christian.

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.880
She said I had this premonition.

00:41:38.880 --> 00:41:41.940
It was kind of like a dream
but it was more of a vision

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:44.250
that your son, she was talking to my mom.

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:47.640
Link was in front of this
huge crowd in a stadium

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:50.160
and it was like he was Billy Graham

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:52.770
and he was like preaching the gospel.

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:54.900
And I was in high school
when she told me this.

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:56.973
My mom relayed it and I was like okay,

00:41:57.914 --> 00:42:01.430
thanks for that pressure.

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:06.160
I don't know maybe she saw
us performing in a venue

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:07.270
and she didn't realize that

00:42:07.270 --> 00:42:09.380
we were just singing a dumb song.

00:42:09.380 --> 00:42:10.550
Oh, so I was there?

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:11.383
You were not there.

00:42:11.383 --> 00:42:13.010
Okay, well it sounds
like a solo act to me.

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:13.980
Yeah.

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:14.880
To be honest with you.

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:15.940
That hasn't happened.

00:42:15.940 --> 00:42:18.510
I mean that that's a
vision, a miracle but...

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:23.510
Well, I have what I would
have called my miracles.

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:26.180
I'm gonna tell my stories.

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:27.013
Okay.

00:42:27.013 --> 00:42:27.846
Right now.

00:42:27.846 --> 00:42:28.679
All right.

00:42:28.679 --> 00:42:30.903
And then I'm gonna tell
you my perspective on them.

00:42:33.160 --> 00:42:38.160
First miracle, 1998, I'm in New York City

00:42:41.269 --> 00:42:44.227
working with Campus Crusade for Christ

00:42:44.227 --> 00:42:46.520
"Summer in the City" is what we called it

00:42:46.520 --> 00:42:50.530
where me and I don't know,
30 40 other college students

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:53.222
worked with the churches in the inner city

00:42:53.222 --> 00:42:55.730
to do basically you were
with a different church

00:42:55.730 --> 00:43:00.050
every single week and doing
whatever outreach and stuff

00:43:00.050 --> 00:43:01.777
they were organizing.

00:43:01.777 --> 00:43:05.780
And interestingly, one of the things

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:09.357
that I was struggling with at the time,

00:43:09.357 --> 00:43:11.761
this is one of my first sort of

00:43:11.761 --> 00:43:14.120
I don't remember exactly
where I learned it

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:15.340
but when I started learning more

00:43:15.340 --> 00:43:17.783
about how the New Testament came together,

00:43:17.783 --> 00:43:19.730
specifically you know the way

00:43:19.730 --> 00:43:22.778
the way the Canon was
decided and the counsels

00:43:22.778 --> 00:43:25.991
of these church fathers getting together

00:43:25.991 --> 00:43:30.130
quite some time after the
the writing of the Bible

00:43:30.130 --> 00:43:33.710
to then kind of determine

00:43:33.710 --> 00:43:35.739
what the books of the Bible would be.

00:43:35.739 --> 00:43:37.980
Yeah, let's not worry

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:39.420
about the book of Thomas.

00:43:39.420 --> 00:43:42.253
Exclude some of those books.

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:47.670
And this was troublesome, right.

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:49.720
For someone who had never
really thought about this

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:52.580
and was just like oh,
the Bible is God's Word,

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:56.050
so you kind of just imagined that it was--

00:43:56.050 --> 00:43:56.883
It was.

00:43:56.883 --> 00:43:58.640
Somebody just you know like I know

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:01.510
I think the the tradition
of the Quran is that

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:05.956
God basically filled Muhammad
and then he wrote it.

00:44:05.956 --> 00:44:08.580
I'm ignorant about that.

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:10.910
But I think it has a little
bit in like the tradition

00:44:10.910 --> 00:44:13.320
of the Book of Mormon I think is that

00:44:14.160 --> 00:44:17.580
God filled the Holy Spirit
kind of came upon Joseph Smith

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:18.520
and got him to write it.

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:21.330
Like that kind of feels like a good thing

00:44:21.330 --> 00:44:22.872
to kind of fall back on

00:44:22.872 --> 00:44:24.483
like it feels a little tighter

00:44:24.483 --> 00:44:28.230
you know from a scripture
standpoint versus like well...

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:30.040
Some people wrote some books.

00:44:30.040 --> 00:44:32.010
We don't know exactly
what their intentions were

00:44:32.010 --> 00:44:32.843
and then--
Let's get

00:44:32.843 --> 00:44:33.676
to the miracle, man.

00:44:33.676 --> 00:44:34.850
So you were doubting?

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:35.683
Right.

00:44:35.683 --> 00:44:37.270
So I started thinking about this

00:44:37.270 --> 00:44:38.670
and had some doubts about it

00:44:38.670 --> 00:44:41.980
and I specifically was
arguing with a Muslim student

00:44:41.980 --> 00:44:42.813
on some college campus in New York,

00:44:42.813 --> 00:44:45.480
I can't remember which one it was.

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:48.160
Not arguing but we're
having a friendly discussion

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:53.160
where he started to attack
the New Testament in this way,

00:44:54.701 --> 00:44:57.500
basically saying what I
was just talking to you

00:44:57.500 --> 00:44:58.580
from a Muslim perspective

00:44:58.580 --> 00:45:00.610
and basically saying how
the Quran is more superior

00:45:00.610 --> 00:45:02.113
because of this and I didn't
have any answers for him

00:45:02.113 --> 00:45:04.670
because I hadn't really looked into it.

00:45:04.670 --> 00:45:07.180
So and of course, I'm in New York,

00:45:07.180 --> 00:45:09.890
it's 1998, I don't really
have access to the internet

00:45:09.890 --> 00:45:11.530
because we didn't have cellphones

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:12.915
and there was no computer anybody had.

00:45:12.915 --> 00:45:15.440
So I didn't really have any
way to research or anything.

00:45:15.440 --> 00:45:16.930
So it just kind of ate at me

00:45:16.930 --> 00:45:19.530
as I continued to do ministry
throughout the summer.

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:23.360
And then one day, we went square-dancing

00:45:23.360 --> 00:45:24.340
because of course square dancing

00:45:24.340 --> 00:45:26.204
was what every college student

00:45:26.204 --> 00:45:29.073
who knew which way was up
was doing in the late '90s

00:45:29.073 --> 00:45:31.140
especially in Christian groups.

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:34.098
Well, we squared-danced like rabbits.

00:45:34.098 --> 00:45:34.931
Amen.

00:45:34.931 --> 00:45:38.500
I just mixed metaphors there
because we couldn't have sex,

00:45:38.500 --> 00:45:39.580
we were square dancing yes.

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:41.870
That's kind of what I was getting
at with the rabbits thing.

00:45:41.870 --> 00:45:44.410
Well, you couldn't grind, so you squared?

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:45.243
Right.

00:45:45.243 --> 00:45:49.880
And so, we went to the
Lincoln Center or something,

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:51.330
I can't remember we were to square dance

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:52.450
and then we had to come back

00:45:52.450 --> 00:45:54.670
and of course we're
using the subway system

00:45:54.670 --> 00:45:56.410
and there's no cell phone or anything

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:58.601
so you're like using
your map that you've got

00:45:58.601 --> 00:46:01.080
and we realized that the subway station

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:02.830
that we need to get on

00:46:02.830 --> 00:46:04.580
the subway we need to
get on to get to a story

00:46:04.580 --> 00:46:07.850
which is where we were living had closed

00:46:07.850 --> 00:46:09.170
because it was so late

00:46:09.170 --> 00:46:11.900
but there was a stop across Central Park

00:46:11.900 --> 00:46:13.430
that we could get on

00:46:13.430 --> 00:46:15.490
but it was only gonna be open
for like 10 more minutes.

00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:17.790
So we all start running
across Central Park,

00:46:17.790 --> 00:46:19.270
not the long way, the short way.

00:46:19.270 --> 00:46:20.954
It's not that hard to do.

00:46:20.954 --> 00:46:22.810
We've got just a little bit of time

00:46:22.810 --> 00:46:24.200
to get to the subway station.

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:25.706
We get there, we go down,

00:46:25.706 --> 00:46:27.850
we move through the turnstile

00:46:27.850 --> 00:46:30.780
and then we get on to the subway

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:34.070
and I go to my seat and right at the seat,

00:46:34.070 --> 00:46:38.550
that I'm about to sit down
at, is a sheet of paper.

00:46:38.550 --> 00:46:39.383
Okay.

00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:42.310
I pick up the sheet of paper

00:46:42.310 --> 00:46:46.550
and it's just like a portion of a paper

00:46:46.550 --> 00:46:50.070
like a term paper like so just text.

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:52.150
Just typed text.
Typed and put out

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:54.390
Not a professional publication.

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:57.580
And it basically begins to explain

00:46:57.580 --> 00:47:02.580
why the New Testament is necessary,

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:08.400
how you couldn't just stop
with the book of Malachi

00:47:09.310 --> 00:47:12.920
but that the Old Testament was calling

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:15.093
for the fulfillment in the New Testament.

00:47:16.550 --> 00:47:19.831
And I took that sheet of
paper and I said thank you God

00:47:19.831 --> 00:47:22.030
for proving yourself to me.

00:47:22.030 --> 00:47:24.020
I'm not gonna doubt about this anymore

00:47:24.020 --> 00:47:25.430
and put it in my journal.

00:47:25.430 --> 00:47:26.820
You stole somebody's paper.

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.072
Big Time miracle, right.

00:47:29.072 --> 00:47:31.660
What is my perspective on that now?

00:47:31.660 --> 00:47:34.720
I told you the story the way
that I've told it many times,

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:36.800
the way I told it to groups of people

00:47:36.800 --> 00:47:37.950
when I was a Christian.

00:47:38.920 --> 00:47:42.560
you know I'm gonna tell
you the rest of the story

00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:43.810
as Paul Harvey would say.

00:47:47.480 --> 00:47:48.536
Because the way I told the story,

00:47:48.536 --> 00:47:51.833
Paul Harvey wrote most
of the New Testament.

00:47:51.833 --> 00:47:53.490
He did.

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:54.823
The Apostle Paul Harvey.

00:47:56.470 --> 00:47:58.710
The way that I would tell that story.

00:47:58.710 --> 00:48:00.485
What'd you leave out?

00:48:00.485 --> 00:48:03.180
Is I told it in a kind
of a sensational way.

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:05.270
Right, I kind of set you
up with what I was doubting

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:07.330
and then I went to this particular seat

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:08.827
that was in the particular
place that I went to

00:48:08.827 --> 00:48:09.870
and there's a sheet of paper

00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:11.810
and it addressed this thing directly.

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:12.824
Yeah.

00:48:12.824 --> 00:48:15.350
The reality of the way that it happened

00:48:15.350 --> 00:48:17.424
Sounds like Ear Biscuits.

00:48:17.424 --> 00:48:19.050
Well, it made a better story that way

00:48:19.050 --> 00:48:20.150
but the reality of the way,

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:23.070
that that's also the way that
I told myself by the way,

00:48:23.070 --> 00:48:25.530
when I would go back to that
miracle time after time.

00:48:25.530 --> 00:48:29.155
But the way that it actually happened,

00:48:29.155 --> 00:48:32.190
everything leading up to
getting on the subway's true

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:33.820
and I was doubting this stuff.

00:48:33.820 --> 00:48:35.520
But we got on the subway.

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:39.833
There was flyers like all
over the multiple seats

00:48:39.833 --> 00:48:42.160
like it wasn't just the
seat that I was going to,

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:43.320
it was everywhere.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:44.153
Okay.

00:48:44.153 --> 00:48:45.656
When I got to it and read it,

00:48:45.656 --> 00:48:49.967
it was someone who is mentally unstable

00:48:49.967 --> 00:48:52.590
had written a bunch of sort

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:55.860
of religiously informed gibberish.

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:56.950
Oh, like a manifesto.

00:48:56.950 --> 00:48:59.477
And in this and it wasn't
just a sheet of paper

00:48:59.477 --> 00:49:02.380
it was a few sheets of paper

00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:05.883
and as I read it,
nothing really made sense

00:49:05.883 --> 00:49:09.120
and it was like this person
seems a little bit off

00:49:09.120 --> 00:49:11.120
but oh but there is a line in here

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:16.120
that says something about and
just like the book of Malachi

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:18.696
is calling out for the book of Matthew

00:49:18.696 --> 00:49:21.090
blah-blah-blah-blah-blah.

